**Shape:** writes share a ~3.6–5.3 GiB/s ceiling (1 client already ~2/3 of
9-way sw-1m; 9-way per-client lockstep 398–402 MiB/s). Reads scale.
NVMe ceiling is 16.7–21.4 GB/s **per host** — this is software, not media.
50g one-job is slower than 9×2g on 1 client (less parallelism), same
ceiling at 4/9.

## Profile a single write

**ptrace is OPEN (verified Sep 1 2026 by live attach on all 15 hosts):**
`kernel.yama.ptrace_scope=0` on node9901, fstor007 and fcstor003–015, so
`strace -p`, `gdb -p`, `vgdb` and LSan all work same-uid. It is **runtime
only** (`sysctl -w`); `/etc/sysctl.d/99-ptrace.conf` still says 2, so it
reverts on reboot — ask the USER to re-apply, do not edit the conf.
`perf trace` is still out (`perf_event_paranoid=2` and
`/sys/kernel/tracing/events` unreadable); `perf record -p` is fine.
Still do NOT restart daemons under strace — the 353 MiB/s
`Disk stats: md0` run was local RAID after the daemons were killed, not a
tracing overhead measurement. Attach to a RUNNING daemon instead. Same-uid:

```
perf record -e cpu-clock -g --call-graph fp -p $(pgrep -x efsd) -o /tmp/perf-efsd.data sleep 20
perf stat -p $(pgrep -x efsd) sleep 20
# same for efs-fuse; then:
timeout 25 perf report --stdio --no-children -n -i /tmp/perf-efsd.data
```

Aug 29 last sw-1m (2199 MiB/s): client **2.7 cores**, 43% blake3 in
`hash_write_fragments` / `dcache_flush_slot_inner`. Primary **0.01 CPUs**
in `perf stat`; on-CPU leftover is `verify_thread` blake3; workers in
`wait_woken`. Write ceiling is off-CPU (lock/RPC wait), not primary hash.
